TASK: Asset pipeline — Kenney kits committed, GLTF loading with shared
materials, initial triangle count measured (plan Phase 2, Task 2.4)

WHAT CHANGED
- Vendored both kits' GLBs as packed, self-contained models:
  src/assets/kits/toy-car-kit (157 models) and city-kit-suburban (40),
  plus each kit's LICENSE.txt and a provenance README (version, source
  URL, download date, re-pack instructions).
- scripts/glb.ts: shared GLB container read/write (JSON+BIN chunks,
  4-byte chunk alignment, header length recomputed).
- scripts/pack-glb-assets.ts: embeds Kenney's external palette reference
  into the BIN chunk and namespaces the texture name per kit.
- scripts/measure-assets.ts: dependency-free measurement of triangles,
  materials, images and true world-space bounds (accessor min/max walked
  through the node hierarchy with three's matrix math).
- src/game/assets/modelRegistry.ts: the only place that decides which
  models ship; `?url` imports so unreferenced kit art never reaches the
  bundle.
- src/game/assets/modelLibrary.ts: fetch-once templates, clone-based
  instancing (shared geometry/material), palette dedupe by name, shadow
  flags, and teardown.
- package.json: `assets:measure`; tsconfig: allowImportingTsExtensions
  (Node 24 type stripping requires explicit .ts in script imports).

WHY
Kenney ships each GLB pointing at an external Textures/colormap.png. A
bundler cannot honour that reference — the sibling PNG is not emitted and
the hashed URL has no such neighbour — so every model would 404 in a
production build. Packing also makes each model one precacheable file and
lets all models in a kit share a single uploaded texture: both kits name
their palette "colormap", so names are namespaced to keep a runtime cache
from handing one kit's palette to the other kit's models.

MEASURED — the budget this task was asked to establish
- Toy Car Kit 1.2: 157 models, 44,294 triangles, 3.91 MiB (packed 5.22).
- City Kit (Suburban) 2.0: 40 models, 30,035 triangles, 2.49 MiB (packed
  2.94). Committed total: 197 models, 74,329 triangles, 8.16 MiB.
- One palette texture per kit (colormap), so 197 models share 2 textures.
- Vehicles ~1,050-1,270 triangles each, 0.52 x 0.45 x 0.88 units.
- Track: plain road tile 1.00 x 0.30 x 1.00; straight 1.00 x 0.30 x 4.40;
  curve 3.00 x 4.40; corner-small 2.70 x 2.70 — i.e. the Toy Car Kit's
  road system is a 4.4-unit toy-track module, not a 1-unit tile grid.
- Suburban buildings 770-2,062 triangles, 1.0-1.8 units wide; trees
  42-98 triangles.
- Projected v1 town (10 houses + road grid + trees/props + 4 vehicles)
  lands near 25k triangles, which is above the 15k figure recorded in
  tech-stack.md and below the plan's stated ~50k starting budget. The
  discrepancy is flagged for the phase checkpoint rather than resolved
  here, since it changes a documented target.

